--TEST--
A file of top-level statements is the whole program: they run in written order, `echo` writes every expression it is given with nothing between them, `print` writes one, and a class is usable above where it is declared (`rule:tooling/echo-always-has-a-sink`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:programs/a-program-is-a-file-of-top-level-statements
// The statements below are the program. Nothing calls them, and there is no
// `main` for them to live in.
echo "first", "\n";
echo "second", "\n";

// `rule:tooling/echo-always-has-a-sink`: `echo` takes any number of
// expressions and writes each one to the sink, with nothing put between them
// and nothing added at the end.
echo "a", "b", "c";
echo "\n";

// `print` writes exactly one expression, to the same place.
print "one line";
print "\n";

// A class is a declaration rather than a step, so a statement above it may
// already use it.
echo Greeter::greet("world"), "\n";

class Greeter {
    public static function greet(string $who): string {
        return "hello, " . $who;
    }
}

// Written order is what decides the answer: the same two statements the other
// way round would print 1 rather than 10.
int $n = 0;
$n = $n + 1;
$n = $n * 10;
echo $n, "\n";
--EXPECT--
first
second
abc
one line
hello, world
10
